==English==
{{was wotd|2006|June|28}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|tantalise||non-Oxford}}

===Etymology===
From {{m|en|Tantalus}} ({{der|en|grc|Τάνταλος}}) in Greek mythology, who was condemned to [[Tartarus]] in the underworld. There, he had to stand for eternity in water that receded from him when he stooped to drink, beneath fruit trees whose branches were always out of reach. Derived as {{suf|en|Tantalus|ize}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtæntəlaɪz/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-tantalize.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-tantalize.ogg|a=AU}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} to [[tease]] (someone) by [[offering]] something [[desirable]] but keeping it out of [[reach]]
# {{lb|en|transitive}} to [[bait]] (someone) by showing something [[desirable]] but leaving them [[unsatisfied]]

====Quotations====
* {{quote-book|en|year=1880|author=w:John Boyle O'Reilly|title=w:Moondyne|chapter=[[s:Moondyne/The Land of the Red Line|The Land of the Red Line]]|passage=They could not bear to be '''tantalized''' nor tortured by the splendid delusion.}}
* {{quote-book|en|year=1884|author=w:Edwin Abbott Abbott|title=w:Flatland|chapter=[[s:Flatland_(first_edition)/Other_Worlds|Other Worlds]]|section=§ 22|passage=All pleasures palled upon me; all sights '''tantalized''' and tempted me to outspoken treason, because I could not but compare what I saw in Two Dimensions with what it really was if seen in Three, and could hardly refrain from making my comparisons aloud.}}
* {{RQ:Crane Red Badge of Courage|15|149|He had been possessed of much fear of his friend, for he saw how easily questionings could make holes in his feelings. Lately, he had assured himself that the altered comrade would not '''tantalize''' him with a persistent curiosity, but he felt certain that during the first period of leisure his friend would ask him to relate his adventures of the previous day.}}
* {{RQ:Fitzgerald Great Gatsby|year=1953|III|page=53|passage=“It was—simply amazing,” she repeated abstractedly. “But I swore I wouldn’t tell it and here I am '''tantalizing''' you.”}}
* {{quote-book|en|year=1936|author=w:H. P. Lovecraft|title=w:At the Mountains of Madness|chapter=IX|chapterurl={{fullurl:s:At the Mountains of Madness/Chapter 9}}|passage=As we threaded our dim way through the labyrinth with the aid of map and compass{{...|—traversing rooms and corridors in every stage of ruin or preservation, clambering up ramps, crossing upper floors and bridges and clambering down again, encountering choked doorways and piles of debris, hastening now and then along finely preserved and uncannily immaculate stretches, taking false leads and retracing our way (in such cases removing the blind paper trail we had left), and once in a while striking the bottom of an open shaft through which daylight poured or trickled down—}}we were repeatedly '''tantalized''' by the sculptured walls along our route.}}

